1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,845
This is MATH 239, video 17 a. The topic is inhomogeneous linear recurrence relations, section 4.3.2 of the course notes.

2
00:00:10,295 --> 00:00:28,964
After this video, you can turn a recurrence with a right side p of n into a formula for the generating series. You can write the right side as a series, and use the Main Theorem to get a formula for g sub n. You can also find a homogeneous recurrence for the same sequence, and use Theorem 4.18.

3
00:00:29,414 --> 00:00:43,671
A linear recurrence relation has this form. g sub n plus a 1 times g sub n minus 1, and so on, up to a d times g sub n minus d, equals p of n. It holds for all n at least N.

4
00:00:44,121 --> 00:00:53,130
Each new term comes from earlier terms. Here, g sub 4 uses g sub 3 and g sub 2. The orange arcs show this step.

5
00:00:53,580 --> 00:01:02,404
When p of n is zero for every n, the relation is homogeneous. Videos 15 and 16 solved these with partial fractions and the Main Theorem.

6
00:01:02,854 --> 00:01:13,860
When p of n is not zero, the relation is inhomogeneous. An example is p of n equals minus n plus 1. This video solves this kind. The method is almost the same.

7
00:01:14,310 --> 00:01:31,865
Here is Example 4.16. g 0 is 1, and g 1 is 2. For n at least 2, g sub n equals g sub n minus 1, plus 2 g sub n minus 2, minus n, plus 1. The green cells are the initial conditions.

8
00:01:32,315 --> 00:01:40,209
n equals 2. g 2 equals 2, plus 2 times 1, minus 2, plus 1. That is 3.

9
00:01:40,659 --> 00:01:49,669
n equals 3. g 3 equals 3, plus 2 times 2, minus 3, plus 1. That is 5.

10
00:01:50,119 --> 00:01:58,106
n equals 4. g 4 equals 5, plus 2 times 3, minus 4, plus 1. That is 8.

11
00:01:58,556 --> 00:02:07,380
n equals 5. g 5 equals 8, plus 2 times 5, minus 5, plus 1. That is 14.

12
00:02:07,830 --> 00:02:16,975
The recurrence fixes every term. The table continues with 25, 47 and 90. The question asks for a formula for g sub n, without the table.

13
00:02:17,425 --> 00:02:22,394
Here is the plan. Start with the recurrence. It holds for all n at least 2.

14
00:02:22,844 --> 00:02:27,627
Multiply both sides by x to the n, and sum over all n at least 2.

15
00:02:28,077 --> 00:02:33,743
The left side becomes an expression in G of x. The right side becomes a known power series.

16
00:02:34,193 --> 00:02:39,823
Solve for G of x. The result is a rational function, a quotient of two polynomials.

17
00:02:40,273 --> 00:02:47,471
Then the Main Theorem gives the form of g sub n. The only new part is the right side. It also becomes a series.

18
00:02:47,921 --> 00:03:05,336
Start with the right side. Multiply p of n by x to the n and sum over n at least 2. Put n equal to j plus 2, so that the sum starts at j equals 0. The top row is the series with coefficients j plus 1: 1, 2, 3, and so on.

19
00:03:05,786 --> 00:03:13,170
With n equal to j plus 2, the term becomes minus j minus 2 plus 1, times x to the j plus 2.

20
00:03:13,620 --> 00:03:26,113
Minus j minus 2 plus 1 is minus j plus 1. Take out x squared and the minus sign. Multiplication by x squared moves every coefficient two places to the right. The purple arrows show the move.

21
00:03:26,563 --> 00:03:43,560
The series of j plus 1 is 1 over 1 minus x squared, by the Binomial Series with t equal to 2. So the right side is minus x squared over 1 minus x, squared. The orange row shows its coefficients: 0, 0, minus 1, minus 2, and so on.

22
00:03:44,010 --> 00:03:51,719
Now the left side. Here is G of x as a row of coefficients: 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, and so on.

23
00:03:52,169 --> 00:03:57,138
Minus x times G of x is the same row, moved one place to the right, with the sign changed.

24
00:03:57,588 --> 00:04:02,836
Minus 2 x squared times G of x is the row moved two places, times minus 2.

25
00:04:03,286 --> 00:04:13,177
Add the three rows, column by column. Column n gives g sub n minus g sub n minus 1 minus 2 g sub n minus 2.

26
00:04:13,627 --> 00:04:25,562
For n at least 2, the recurrence says this column sum is minus n plus 1. These are the green cells. Columns 0 and 1 are not covered by the recurrence. They give 1 and 1, from the initial conditions.

27
00:04:26,012 --> 00:04:35,022
So 1 minus x minus 2 x squared, times G of x, equals 1 plus x, plus the series of the right side.

28
00:04:35,472 --> 00:04:56,184
The notes write the same steps with sums. Split the sum into three sums. In the second sum put j equal to n minus 1. In the third sum put k equal to n minus 2. Take out x and 2 x squared. The first row shows the first sum. It is G of x without the terms for n equal 0 and 1. The red cells are missing.

29
00:04:56,634 --> 00:05:08,522
The second sum is G of x without the term for j equal 0. The third sum is all of G of x. So each sum is G of x minus its missing first terms.

30
00:05:08,972 --> 00:05:12,688
Put in g 0 equal to 1 and g 1 equal to 2.

31
00:05:13,138 --> 00:05:23,447
Collect the terms. The left side is 1 minus x minus 2 x squared, times G of x, minus 1, minus x. This agrees with the table on the last slide.

32
00:05:23,897 --> 00:05:28,309
Set the left side equal to the right side. This is one equation for G of x.

33
00:05:28,759 --> 00:05:31,220
Move 1 plus x to the right side.

34
00:05:31,670 --> 00:05:35,711
Write the right side over the common denominator 1 minus x, squared.

35
00:05:36,161 --> 00:05:50,557
Multiply out. 1 plus x, times 1 minus x squared, is 1 minus x minus x squared plus x cubed. Subtract x squared. The numerator is 1 minus x minus 2 x squared plus x cubed.

36
00:05:51,007 --> 00:06:04,242
Factor 1 minus x minus 2 x squared as 1 plus x, times 1 minus 2 x. Divide. G of x is the numerator over 1 plus x, times 1 minus x squared, times 1 minus 2 x.

37
00:06:04,692 --> 00:06:14,491
Check the result. Expand the fraction as a power series. The coefficients are 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 14, 25, 47. They agree with the table.

38
00:06:14,941 --> 00:06:31,242
G of x is a rational function, so the Main Theorem 4.14 applies. The numerator has degree 3, and the denominator has degree 4. The numerator degree is smaller, so the polynomial R of x is zero. The form holds for all n at least 0.

39
00:06:31,692 --> 00:06:39,958
The factor 1 plus x gives the inverse root minus 1 with multiplicity 1. It gives a constant A times minus 1 to the n.

40
00:06:40,408 --> 00:06:51,228
The factor 1 minus x squared gives the inverse root 1 with multiplicity 2. It gives a polynomial of degree at most 1, B plus C n, times 1 to the n.

41
00:06:51,678 --> 00:07:00,874
The factor 1 minus 2 x gives D times 2 to the n. So g sub n equals A times minus 1 to the n, plus B plus C n, plus D times 2 to the n.

42
00:07:01,324 --> 00:07:12,237
Put n equal to 0, 1, 2 and 3 into the form. Each value of g sub n gives one linear equation. There are four equations and four unknowns.

43
00:07:12,687 --> 00:07:24,250
Solve the system. A is minus 1 over 12. B is 3 over 4. C is 1 over 2. D is 1 over 3. The rows on the right show each term for n from 0 to 5.

44
00:07:24,700 --> 00:07:33,663
Add the four rows. Column by column, the sum is 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 14. These are the terms of the sequence.

45
00:07:34,113 --> 00:07:40,894
The columns 0 to 3 were used to find the constants. The columns 4 and 5 are a free check. They agree with the table.

46
00:07:41,344 --> 00:07:58,965
Write the result with one denominator. g sub n equals one twelfth of 2 to the n plus 2, plus 6 n, plus 9, minus minus 1 to the n. This holds for all n at least 0. For example, n equals 8 gives 1080 over 12, which is 90.

47
00:07:59,415 --> 00:08:13,858
Example 4.17 goes back. Multiply out the denominator of G of x. It is 1 minus 3 x plus x squared plus 3 x cubed minus 2 x to the 4. The top row is G of x.

48
00:08:14,308 --> 00:08:30,868
Theorem 4.8 says: Q of x times G of x equals the numerator P of x. The table shows Q of x times G of x as five shifted rows. Each row is G of x, moved k places, times the coefficient of x to the k in Q.

49
00:08:31,318 --> 00:08:37,824
Add the columns. The sums are 1, minus 1, minus 2, 1, and then zeros.

50
00:08:38,274 --> 00:08:48,537
The first four sums are the coefficients of the numerator, 1 minus x minus 2 x squared plus x cubed. From column 4 on, every sum is 0.

51
00:08:48,987 --> 00:09:11,118
A zero column sum is a homogeneous recurrence. g sub n minus 3 g sub n minus 1 plus g sub n minus 2 plus 3 g sub n minus 3 minus 2 g sub n minus 4 equals 0, for n at least 4. The initial conditions are 1, 2, 3, 5. So the same sequence also satisfies a homogeneous relation.

52
00:09:11,568 --> 00:09:20,159
Examples 4.16 and 4.17 show a general fact. Suppose the series of the right side is a rational function.

53
00:09:20,609 --> 00:09:29,526
Then the same steps make G of x a rational function. The left side gives Q of x times G of x minus a polynomial. The right side is a rational function.

54
00:09:29,976 --> 00:09:41,493
So the sequence also satisfies a homogeneous linear recurrence, by Theorem 4.8. We are back in the case of videos 15 and 16. Theorem 4.18 states this in general.

55
00:09:41,943 --> 00:09:56,153
The notes give a name to the functions in the Main Theorem. A function q from the natural numbers to the complex numbers is polyexp if it is a sum of terms. Each term is a polynomial in n times a number lambda to the n.

56
00:09:56,603 --> 00:10:09,421
Examples. A constant 5 is 5 times 1 to the n. n squared times 3 to the n is one term. n plus 1 times 2 to the n, minus 4 times minus 1 to the n, has two terms.

57
00:10:09,871 --> 00:10:19,112
cos of n theta is polyexp. It is the sum of e to the i theta n and e to the minus i theta n, over 2. The numbers lambda can be complex.

58
00:10:19,562 --> 00:10:27,178
The square root of n is not polyexp. The notes call this name not standard. They use it to state Theorem 4.18.

59
00:10:27,628 --> 00:10:40,353
A function is eventually polyexp if the formula holds for all n at least some M. The first few values can be different. The top row is the sequence h of Example 4.15, from video 16.

60
00:10:40,803 --> 00:10:50,741
The formula from Example 4.15 is n minus 5, times 2 to the n minus 4, minus 3 times minus 1 to the n. The second row shows its values.

61
00:10:51,191 --> 00:11:02,569
For n equal to 0, 1 and 2, the formula gives fractions. They differ from h. From n equal to 3 on, the values agree. So h is eventually polyexp, with M equal to 3.

62
00:11:03,019 --> 00:11:10,078
In this language, the Main Theorem says: if G of x is rational, then g sub n is eventually polyexp.

63
00:11:10,528 --> 00:11:34,337
Theorem 4.18 collects the results. For a sequence g, four statements are equivalent. Statement a: g satisfies a homogeneous linear recurrence. Statement b: g satisfies a linear recurrence whose right side is eventually polyexp. Statement c: G of x is rational. Statement d: g sub n is eventually polyexp.

64
00:11:34,787 --> 00:11:37,852
Theorem 4.8 shows that a and c are equivalent.

65
00:11:38,302 --> 00:11:45,407
The Main Theorem 4.14 shows that c implies d. Exercise 4.11 shows that d implies c.

66
00:11:45,857 --> 00:11:49,526
a implies b, because zero is a polyexp function.

67
00:11:49,976 --> 00:12:02,236
The last step is b implies c. The notes start this proof, but mark it as under construction. Example 4.16 shows the idea: the right side becomes a rational series, so G of x is rational.

68
00:12:02,686 --> 00:12:16,246
Most right sides use a few series. A constant c for all n at least N gives c x to the N over 1 minus x. With N equal 2 and c equal 5, the row is 0, 0, 5, 5, and so on.

69
00:12:16,696 --> 00:12:21,619
r to the n gives 1 over 1 minus r x. Here r is 3.

70
00:12:22,069 --> 00:12:29,406
n plus 1 gives 1 over 1 minus x, squared. This is the Binomial Series with t equal to 2.

71
00:12:29,856 --> 00:12:36,869
n gives x over 1 minus x, squared. It is the row of n plus 1, moved one place to the right.

72
00:12:37,319 --> 00:12:54,734
n squared gives x times 1 plus x, over 1 minus x, cubed. Exercise 4.10 derives this. Remember: if the recurrence starts at N, the series uses only n at least N. Subtract any terms with n less than N.

73
00:12:55,184 --> 00:13:05,029
Exercise 4.10 parts a and b. Pause the video and try it. Write n squared with binomial coefficients, then find the series of n squared.

74
00:13:05,479 --> 00:13:20,572
Put n equal to 0, 1 and 2. n plus 2 choose 2 is 1, 3 and 6. This gives three equations. The solution is A equals 2, B equals minus 3, C equals 1.

75
00:13:21,022 --> 00:13:27,709
The three rows show 2 times n plus 2 choose 2, minus 3 times n plus 1, and plus 1.

76
00:13:28,159 --> 00:13:43,252
Add them. The sum is 0, 1, 4, 9, 16, 25. This is n squared. Both sides are polynomials of degree 2 in n, and they agree at three points, so they agree for all n.

77
00:13:43,702 --> 00:14:02,743
Now use the Binomial Series. n plus 2 choose 2 gives 1 over 1 minus x, cubed. n plus 1 gives 1 over 1 minus x, squared. 1 gives 1 over 1 minus x. Over the common denominator, the sum is x times 1 plus x, over 1 minus x, cubed.

78
00:14:03,193 --> 00:14:14,031
Here is a practice question of the same type. Pause the video and try it. u 0 is 2, and u sub n is 2 u sub n minus 1 plus n, for n at least 1.

79
00:14:14,481 --> 00:14:19,914
First compute some terms. u 1 is 2 times 2, plus 1, which is 5.

80
00:14:20,364 --> 00:14:23,755
u 2 is 2 times 5, plus 2, which is 12.

81
00:14:24,205 --> 00:14:31,585
u 3 is 2 times 12, plus 3, which is 27. These values check the answer at the end.

82
00:14:32,035 --> 00:14:44,760
Multiply by x to the n and sum over n at least 1. The left side is U of x minus 2, minus 2 x U of x. The right side is the series of n. It is x over 1 minus x, squared.

83
00:14:45,210 --> 00:15:04,436
Move the terms with U to the left. 1 minus 2 x, times U of x, equals 2 plus x over 1 minus x squared. Over the common denominator, the numerator is 2 times 1 minus 2 x plus x squared, plus x. That is 2 minus 3 x plus 2 x squared.

84
00:15:04,886 --> 00:15:14,592
Divide by 1 minus 2 x. U of x is 2 minus 3 x plus 2 x squared, over 1 minus 2 x, times 1 minus x squared.

85
00:15:15,042 --> 00:15:31,946
The Main Theorem gives the form. The inverse root 2 gives A times 2 to the n. The inverse root 1 with multiplicity 2 gives B plus C n. The numerator degree 2 is less than the denominator degree 3, so the form holds for all n at least 0.

86
00:15:32,396 --> 00:15:37,458
Use u 0, u 1 and u 2. This gives three equations.

87
00:15:37,908 --> 00:15:48,682
The solution is A equals 4, B equals minus 2, C equals minus 1. So u sub n equals 2 to the n plus 2, minus n, minus 2.

88
00:15:49,132 --> 00:16:05,665
Check with values that were not used. The table adds the three terms for n from 0 to 5. n equals 3 gives 32 minus 3 minus 2, which is 27. n equals 4 gives 58, and the recurrence also gives 2 times 27 plus 4, which is 58.

89
00:16:06,115 --> 00:16:22,880
A second practice question. Pause the video and try it. v 0 is 0, v 1 is 1, and v sub n is v sub n minus 1 plus 2 v sub n minus 2 plus 3, for n at least 2. The first terms are 0, 1, 4, 9, 20, 41.

90
00:16:23,330 --> 00:16:29,367
For example, v 3 is 4, plus 2 times 1, plus 3, which is 9.

91
00:16:29,817 --> 00:16:46,860
Multiply by x to the n and sum over n at least 2. The left side is V minus x, minus x V, minus 2 x squared V. The right side is the constant 3 for n at least 2. That is 3 x squared over 1 minus x.

92
00:16:47,310 --> 00:16:59,478
Collect V on the left. The right side is x plus 3 x squared over 1 minus x. Over one denominator the numerator is x minus x squared plus 3 x squared, which is x plus 2 x squared.

93
00:16:59,928 --> 00:17:14,138
Factor 1 minus x minus 2 x squared as 1 plus x, times 1 minus 2 x. So V of x is x plus 2 x squared, over 1 minus x, times 1 plus x, times 1 minus 2 x. This is part a.

94
00:17:14,588 --> 00:17:21,879
Part b. Multiply out the denominator: 1 minus 2 x minus x squared plus 2 x cubed.

95
00:17:22,329 --> 00:17:50,750
Theorem 4.8 gives the homogeneous relation. v sub n minus 2 v sub n minus 1 minus v sub n minus 2 plus 2 v sub n minus 3 equals 0, for n at least 3. The numerator has degree 2, so the initial conditions are v 0, v 1 and v 2: 0, 1 and 4. Check: v 3 equals 2 times 4, plus 1, minus 0, which is 9.

96
00:17:51,200 --> 00:18:05,597
Part c. The inverse roots are 1, minus 1 and 2, each with multiplicity 1. So v sub n equals A plus B times minus 1 to the n, plus C times 2 to the n. Use n equal 0, 1 and 2.

97
00:18:06,047 --> 00:18:28,245
The solution is A equals minus 3 over 2, B equals 1 over 6, C equals 4 over 3. So v sub n equals 2 to the n plus 3, minus 9, plus minus 1 to the n, all over 6. The table shows the three terms and their sum: 0, 1, 4, 9, 20, 41. This agrees with the recurrence.

98
00:18:28,695 --> 00:18:50,847
Here is the written solution in the form the course expects, with Practice 1 as the example. Step 1: multiply the recurrence by x to the n, and sum over the values of n where it holds. Step 2: write the left side with U of x and the initial conditions, and write the right side in closed form. Name the series that you use.

99
00:18:51,297 --> 00:19:03,511
Step 3: solve for the generating series and factor the denominator. Step 4: state the form from the Main Theorem, and say for which n it holds. Compare the degrees of the numerator and the denominator.

100
00:19:03,961 --> 00:19:09,440
Step 5: fit the constants with linear equations. Then check one more term with the recurrence.

101
00:19:09,890 --> 00:19:26,450
To recap. An inhomogeneous recurrence has a nonzero right side p of n. Multiply by x to the n and sum. The left side gives Q of x times G of x minus the initial terms. The right side is a known series.

102
00:19:26,900 --> 00:19:37,674
Then G of x is rational, and the Main Theorem gives the form of g sub n. Fit the constants. The denominator of G of x also gives a homogeneous recurrence for the same sequence.

103
00:19:38,124 --> 00:19:50,895
A polyexp function is a sum of polynomials times powers. Theorem 4.18 says the four conditions are equivalent. Video 17 b covers quadratic recurrences and the Catalan numbers.
